  "worked_solution": "Let $u=z+4.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}z=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$z+5=u+1.$\n\nNow we can solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{z+5}{\\sqrt{z+4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{u+1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{u}{\\sqrt{u}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u+\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u+\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+2\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{(z+4)^{3}}}{3}+2\\sqrt{z+4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
